`context/renders/materials/<syllabus>/<concept>.<lang>.md` is a **material**: the guide a
learner studies. `<concept>.<lang>.slide.md` next to it is the deck. It is derived from a wiki
page and its lesson but governed by neither lane: no frontmatter, no kind, no lint, no
derivation gate. Its first line is the header `sdt context wiki render --check` reads.

Read this module when you write or revise a guide, after `instructions/lesson.md` (the
example and the transfer) and `instructions/practice.md` (the cases). `instructions/course.md`
owns the sequence.

## The brief

You do not start from the wiki page. You start from a brief with six fields:

- **Reader**: who reads, and what they already know.
- **Decision**: the concrete choice they must be able to make afterwards.
- **Facts available**: the only source of content — the claims, in prose, without ids.
- **Cases**: the scenes from the practice notes, with names and figures, used as given.
- **Shape**: the section order, when it differs from the default.
- **Measure**: the word range and any other limit.

You may also receive the existing guide. It is a source of facts and a trace, not a model to
follow: when it contradicts the brief, the brief wins. If a field is missing or two fields
contradict each other, ask one short question before writing.

## The shape

Unless the brief says otherwise, a guide carries these parts, in this order.

1. **The decision.** A few lines in the second person that put the reader in front of a
   concrete choice. No definitions, no list of facts; the facts come later, where they belong.
2. **The concepts**, each with the point from which it is read or used, and — where the brief
   asks — what it cannot see or cannot say.
3. **A worked example**, with figures that add up.
4. **The brief's case**, used to apply the concepts, not as an anecdote.
5. **An exercise**: three numbered steps, in order, with a concrete result (a table, a line,
   a justified choice).
6. **The check**: one line after the exercise naming the evaluation bank of the page and its
   items, two per objective; the key stays in the bank. It names items, never answers, and it
   is generated from the bank, so a count that does not match the objectives leaves it out.
7. **What it presupposes**: the pages, techniques or terms the reader must already know, and
   where the guide sits in the course. It is not a maxim.

Never state what the page lacks, and never name a successor that has no material.

## Style

- Sentences under 25 words, one idea each; paragraphs of two to four sentences.
- Second person singular, direct and sober. No emphatic adverbs and no rhetorical
  superlatives.
- A foreign term stays in its language, with the Italian gloss in parentheses at first use.
  Do not redefine a term the corpus uses without defining it.
- A general principle — a law, a maxim, a rule — appears only with the case that shows it, in
  the same section. Never alone, before the case.
- Every section answers a question the reader is actually asking, and its title says so.
- Use the cases' names and figures exactly as given.

For tone, not content:

> Lunedì il titolare del negozio ti chiede perché gli incassi di ottobre sono scesi. Hai
> l'elenco degli scontrini e un'ora di tempo. Devi scegliere che cosa guardare e che cosa dire.
> Non devi imparare tutti gli indicatori: sono la materia prima da cui scegli.

## Facts

- The brief is the only source. Do not add figures, names, roles, motives, sectors or details
  it does not contain; do not give a concept a meaning or a property the brief does not give.
- You may connect facts with linking sentences. When the link is an inference, write it
  cautiously and report it at delivery.
- Do not draw from a case a conclusion stronger than its data. If the data do not say
  something got worse, do not write that it got worse.
- Do not repeat the same fact in two sections.
- Do not fix thresholds, values or criteria the brief leaves to the reader.

## Figures

A figure earns its place only when it carries a relation the prose cannot state in one
sentence. The medium follows the content:

- a relation, a flow, a sequence or a decomposition → a Mermaid fence;
- values over categories or time → `xychart`, with the same values in a table;
- parts of a whole → `pie`; points on two axes → `quadrantChart`; flows with quantities →
  `sankey`;
- a scatter whose points carry a size → a table; a positioning matrix → a small SVG;
- a dashboard → a table of its tiles and bands, never the screenshot;
- a screenshot, a photo or a decoration → left out, with the reason recorded.

**No values, no chart.** A chart whose values the brief does not carry is dropped, not
invented: the source's dashboard is its own system, and a guide that borrows its numbers
teaches the reader nothing about theirs. Cite the figure to the span that carries it; shape
defers to `instructions/diagram.md` when the project has one.

## The binder

The text is written without markers. After the draft, a binder pass:

1. maps each sentence to a claim, stamping `<!-- claim=<page>#<id> digest=<sha> -->`;
2. marks the lesson's example and transfer with `<!-- lesson=<page>#worked-example -->` and
   `#transfer`, a gloss with `<!-- gloss=<term> -->`, and each objective section with
   `<!-- objective=N -->` exactly once;
3. lists the sentences that have no claim — the inferences and the case prose — so the delivery
   can say which they are;
4. writes the header on the first line: concept, syllabus, language, classification, and one
   claim id with its digest per asserted claim.

The writing context never sees the markers. `sdt context wiki render --check` is the gate on
the result, and the ledger beside the artefact records what the binder could not bind.

## Checks before delivery

- Recompute every arithmetic operation and every reading of a percentage in words: 2 out of 36
  is about six per hundred, not two per hundred. Correct the errors inherited from the existing
  guide too.
- Check that the cases' sums add up.
- Count the words against the brief's range, and the sentences against 25 words.
- Check that every foreign term is glossed at first use.
- Check that no marker, HTML comment or LaTeX is left in the draft — the binder adds the first.
- Check that every fact appears in the brief; if not, remove it or flag it.
- Check that the presupposition names prerequisites, not a maxim.
- Check that every item id in the check line exists in the page's assessment, and that the
  two-per-objective count still holds.

Deliver the files, then three things: the final lengths, the errors corrected in the existing
version, and the places where you interpreted the facts beyond the brief, so the reviewer
knows where to look.

## The references

Three guides written and revised by a reader are the reference texts: `four-key-metrics.it.md`,
`architecture-measurement.it.md` and `kpi-value-tree.it.md`, under
`context/renders/materials/software-architect/`. Read one before writing. The counterexample —
the same guide before the revision, with the two readers' defects — is recorded in
`context/notes/20261107-152633-material-doctrine-ab-kpi-value-tree.md`.

Imitate their voice and their shape, never their content.
